  Coverage:
  - defaults returned from empty storage
  - [1mset → construct a fresh instance from the same storage → value reads back[0m (this is the actual "persists across
    sessions" claim, and the reason the injected backend matters)
  - corrupt JSON → defaults, no throw
  - one invalid value → that key defaults, others survive
  - unknown [38;5;153mversion[39m → defaults
  - [38;5;153mset[39m on unknown key throws; [38;5;153mset[39m on invalid value throws
  - turning [38;5;153mrememberUsername[39m off clears [38;5;153mlastUsername[39m
  - throwing storage → in-memory fallback, no crash

  The [38;5;153mapp.js[39m wiring isn't unit-tested — that needs a DOM, which you declined. I'll verify it by loading the page,
  toggling both preferences, reloading, and confirming they stick, and I'll report that as a manual check rather than a
  passing test.

